# Native UI — Interdía

**Goal:** One shared UI path on iOS, Android, and web. Style with NativeWind; use React Native primitives. No platform-specific UI files or `@expo/ui`.

## Preferred stack

### Styling — NativeWind

- Use **[NativeWind](https://www.nativewind.dev/)** for layout, spacing, typography scale, and colors via `className`.
- Prefer utility classes over large `StyleSheet.create` blocks for app chrome and screens.
- Import CSS-enabled primitives from `src/tw` (re-exports `react-native-css/components`) when using `className`.
- Keep Tailwind theme tokens in CSS (`src/global.css` / `@theme`); avoid one-off hardcoded colors when a token exists.
- Platform font token overrides in `global.css` (`@media ios` / `@media android`) are fine — they feed NativeWind theme vars.
- For setup/troubleshooting, use the Expo agent skill **`expo-tailwind-setup`** (`.agents/skills/expo-tailwind-setup`).

### Controls — shared RN primitives

- Use React Native primitives (`Pressable`, `TextInput`, `FlatList`, etc.) and React Navigation for chrome.
- Icons: `@expo/vector-icons`.
- Keep the same components and layout on every platform. Day-to-day development targets **Expo Go** via `expo start` (not `expo run:ios` / `expo run:android`).

## Do not

- Do **not** add `.ios.tsx` / `.android.tsx` (or `.ios.ts` / `.android.ts`) UI modules for **app screens**
- Do **not** use [`@expo/ui`](https://docs.expo.dev/versions/latest/sdk/ui/) (SwiftUI / Jetpack Compose) in app screens
- Do **not** branch app UI on `process.env.EXPO_OS` or `Platform.OS` except for rare unavoidable native APIs
- Do **not** implement Material Design 3 as a design system
- Do **not** use the `material-3` agent skill for Interdía UI
- Do **not** add `react-native-paper` components or themes

## Exception — home-screen widgets only

iOS WidgetKit layouts **must** use `expo-widgets` + `@expo/ui/swift-ui` with the `'widget'` directive under `src/features/widget-ios/`. That is the only allowed `@expo/ui` usage. Shared prop builders stay in `src/features/widgets/` (no SwiftUI). Android widgets stay on `react-native-android-widget` under `src/features/widget-android/`.
